Expose libsecp256k1's `ndata` parameter in `secp256k1_ecdsa_sign()`, enabling consumers(like https://github.com/ACINQ/bitcoin-kmp) to implement low-R signature grinding ([Bitcoin Core PR #13666](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/13666)).

Currently the `ndata` and `noncefp` args are hardcoded to `NULL`. This adds an optional `ndata: ByteArray?` parameter (default `null`) that gets passed through to the RFC 6979 nonce function, preserving full backward compatibility.

  ## Usage: Low-R grinding

Low-R signatures save 1 byte in DER encoding by ensuring R's first byte has MSB=0. With this change, consumers can grind for low-R:

  ```kotlin
  fun signLowR(message: ByteArray, privkey: ByteArray): ByteArray {
      var counter = 0
      while (true) {
          val ndata = if (counter == 0) null
              else ByteArray(32).also { it[0] = (counter and 0xff).toByte() }
          val sig = Secp256k1.sign(message, privkey, ndata)
          // R is the first 32 bytes of the compact signature
          if (sig[0].toInt() and 0x80 == 0) return sig
          counter++
      }
  }
```

This follows the same pattern already used for `signSchnorr(data, sec, auxrand32)`
